name hatecheck-annotations
# HateCheck's README, of "test_suite_annotations.csv" and "all_annotations.csv":
#   label_[1:10] "The label provided for the test case by a given annotator. We recruited and trained a team of ten
#   annotators. Each test case was annotated by exactly five annotators."
#   count_label_h "The number of annotators who labeled a given test case as hateful."   count_label_nh, likewise
#   label_annot_maj "The majority label."
# Each of the ten label columns is an annotator, and its field what that annotator says of the case: nothing is
# written between the case and the label. The other columns are said of the case by the set.
# The case is its test case, the sentence; case_id and templ_id are keys.
# The two counts are what the ten label columns already say, counted: read by nothing.
match all_annotations.csv test_suite_annotations.csv

tier row by \n
header
quoted
tier field by ,

thing row test_case
key row case_id
key row templ_id
omit count_label_h count_label_nh
content test_case functionality label_gold label_annot_maj label_*
# "**functionality** The shorthand for the functionality tested by the test case." and "**label_gold** The gold
# standard label (hateful/non-hateful) of the test case." (README, "See above"): values of the set's own vocabularies.
value - functionality label_gold
# "**label_annot_maj** The majority label."
relation "The majority label" label_annot_maj
attest row functionality label_gold label_annot_maj
voices row label_1 label_2 label_3 label_4 label_5 label_6 label_7 label_8 label_9 label_10
